#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087360 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd63cb30-a236-546e-82ac-08bce09c88f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "8ab1df94-0c4f-5720-a9b6-939dba0ab9c6" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1219" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=4^{x}-1$." :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y=4^{x}{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/46dd42e2c001e441acef975d236e8368.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y=4^x-1$, we take the graph of $y=4^x$ and shift it $1$ unit down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0)$, and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/75376b1492dfea9adebd588fbff21737.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087361 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79daf9dd-814d-562b-b3bc-b066bd2dfb90" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "65c814f8-df2f-549d-9467-c6dda6890bb3" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1220" :question/problem "The graph below shows the curve $y=3^{x}+c$. What is the value of $c$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/c96ce14cb7e8d6abd52346f6c446bc92.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y=3^x$ is $y=0$. The vertical translation\n\n$$\ny=3^x+c\n$$\n\nshifts the asymptote to $y=c$.\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2$. Therefore, $c=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087362 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f73207f4-4661-55f1-acfb-cc5fe6ba9316" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1221" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3$." :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y=3^{x}$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/a9e5c91e185135efce8960d4a81e3626.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)$. To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $5$. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5)$, but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/db1ae2508e49de5eaaedad1694025119.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3$. To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,5)$ to $(0,2)$, and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/4da5afb2a94cb83ada4a74de870a1159.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087363 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "62f7d6e1-61ec-5f3d-8cdd-4da0c90c7ccf" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1222" :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=k(6^{x})+c$. Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/9dbe1a3f16086ee26e329717d5be9e2a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve\n\n$$\ny=k(6^{x})+c\n$$\n\nis $y=c$. In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4$, so $c=4$.\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k(6^{x})+4\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y$. The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6)$, so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= k(6^{x}) + 4 \\\\\n6 &= k(6^{0}) + 4 \\\\\n6 &= k(1) + 4 \\\\\n6 &= k + 4 \\\\\n2 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is\n\n$$\ny=2(6^{x})+4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd63cb30-a236-546e-82ac-08bce09c88f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33ebca09-3e3e-5838-b5d1-70a28c673eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/c5bd7d5b01a3e21124c941bb41af862f564b3f28fc91ee0b291364f9d9729933.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243128} {:answer/id #uuid "7e414d71-1b24-55ff-bf4e-ce9dc9bd9ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/7bbf30d122880d180f8dd2bd1ac61444eb6229c5b20173f860a9c98bf7c4d94a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243129} {:answer/id #uuid "b9091d7a-1c54-5740-9934-9d2abc0432bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/ed0fd77369cb0536c80bc76cdc9cf252539db844b5cb6944a5df89214ef336a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243130} {:answer/id #uuid "c931d7e8-d872-5728-bfcf-7215339d0b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/974bccb96e795348f35b985183b0a72f64ef6d1fd5a4f08f1b62a99f7eaec58b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243131} {:answer/id #uuid "f43429cf-2257-5072-87f6-68a068d7034b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/7781e3bad8901d9207dfaaf4bbf64efeadfeac77dd8dc3721a8efed4dd86b4ef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33ebca09-3e3e-5838-b5d1-70a28c673eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/refe