n.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5100b042-b82c-5834-8756-fafba09daf0e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-26815" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={6}^{x}-0.75?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={6}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/29d270676a48f4cf40625ef3ec50b0323c9c99389b2a7d080881727eda28de56.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={6}^{x}-0.75,$ we take the graph of $y={6}^{x}$ and shift it $0.75$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0.25),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-0.75.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/3b141be99e5c01c70d6f6f04f4adf06741f1dc0fcef80c73b52e3dba1ae44a87.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c722140-882f-5084-8bb5-0d1f24754314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=2$ and $c=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243177} {:answer/id #uuid "abe0c8e0-84b9-5cc9-a31c-f813433f1d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-2$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243178} {:answer/id #uuid "33a25ec1-82fa-5195-a165-b8dd4dde1293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=0$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243179} {:answer/id #uuid "f278ee7c-64e5-5ec3-ac51-984148937a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=0$ and $c=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243180} {:answer/id #uuid "2270eb27-4010-5720-aa37-15e8d324c364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=3$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2270eb27-4010-5720-aa37-15e8d324c364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=3$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243181} :answer-field/id #uuid "def1725a-e53b-5370-ae60-f14121a6a98a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50d9e785-c783-5e7b-a19a-46b5b93eacd8" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-29233" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/e20c8b73d3e46aa90c5d37a30d8343c98d6c6b0f3f17a232580583a8ae812125.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$ Find the value of $k$ and $c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2,$ so $c=-2.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})-2.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})-2 \\\\ 1 & =k({2}^{0})-2 \\\\ 1 & =k(1)-2 \\\\ 1 & =k-2 \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=3$ and $c=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d580471-c03d-55e5-9634-d181285c1113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5.5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243184} {:answer/id #uuid "012e8bbd-148c-5705-beb6-74d5457cba4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10.5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243185} {:answer/id #uuid "732c8a0c-8c9b-58ed-87df-f1edb381041c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,11.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243186} {:answer/id #uuid "00b1d9ff-e450-5ee7-9582-eeeafa197e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243187} {:answer/id #uuid "86a56d94-543b-5e19-8c4c-999be94bb974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00b1d9ff-e450-5ee7-9582-eeeafa197e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243187} :answer-field/id #uuid "f62e9fd0-d581-5e28-a0ab-9c623924ddf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40561353-6ae6-56d7-a244-8d424aca1309" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-30269" :question/problem "For the curve $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={1.5}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=2.5({1.5}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2.5\\cdot 1=2.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2.5+8=10.5.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.5({1.5}^{0})+8 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 1+8 \\\\  & =2.5+8 \\\\  & =10.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,10.5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "895794fe-c257-5933-b561-b3993c250b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(11, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085329} {:answer/id #uuid "c279ef11-cf95-515a-9293-05231b09e412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085330} {:answer/id #uuid "de9ab1fa-f979-5cb8-90d3-6e2516192156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085331} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc144a1-f709-55b3-9f07-9b6d074a0f05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085332} {:answer/id #uuid "56a5658c-31cb-55b9-8e3e-4da9072c1fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de9ab1fa-f979-5cb8-90d3-6e2516192156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085331} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f2be1a2-87ef-5ec5-a1d8-5411e8273458" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9e76cd0-6e63-52b6-ad92-c0e43ec54487" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-30270" :question/problem "For the curve $y = 5(6^{x}) + 2$, find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={6}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=5({6}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=5\\cdot 1=5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=5+2=7.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5({6}^{0})+2 \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =5+2 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=5({6}^{x})+2$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dee014a-305f-5cde-96a6-368d4aacc6a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243191} {:answer/id #uuid "0f295deb-2409-5200-b653-9d5a7a21f58f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243192} {:answer/id #uuid "c0678435-2ecb-5d3c-a058-36b556afcf9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243193} {:answer/id #uuid "4e851679-8431-5588-a655-57dcbfc9741a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243194} {:answer/id #uuid "9fbf5137-c803-5553-ba3b-18df4bc5a91e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f295deb-2409-5200-b653-9d5a7a21f58f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243192} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a493aef-cb5b-57bb-b771-f9d7b70c1586" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95525d30-4708-5ca7-9a4e-37a9e46fc742" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-30873" :question/problem "For the curve $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={10}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=1.5({10}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=1.5\\cdot 1=1.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8.5$ units down, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=1.5-8.5=-7.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.5({10}^{0})-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot 1-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5-8.5 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2673e6f6-6cc5-5ef8-84bb-0f66fe92cb9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/eed5f150b826a708834eb9d7c4595cbdcc0113842fc0b172c1cf11e65681b199.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243198} {:answer/id #uuid "cb744e0b-4ee6-5415-ab50-f3e815f4d529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/d5c68724af1732ee023599ce2969c165dfc68b8e198e14cebbc1262bbf1bd881.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243199} {:answer/id #uuid "7941b161-26ae-598c-a669-dc9a40eceee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/752c0102b1c019f5bc13673b0107627bf58954fb5ef5b80d3b43e33e5f46f55d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243200} {:answer/id #uuid "78f6dae0-7c85-555b-858a-3711c72c3ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/580a0d2a241a867159827d1570d20bb120e70297fef4b68c103fc580bbb2b224.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243201} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b9237c-5f2b-537f-88e3-7f0ab1a84a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/960ebb31bb1330c7def20222f012e2c89d0f2f4f588cabb914c42c60571dfcdc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7b9237c-5f2b-537f-88e3-7f0ab1a84a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/960ebb31bb1330c7def20222f012e2c89d0f2f4f588cabb914c42c60571dfcdc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243202} :answer-field/id #uuid "8845fe8d-61a6-5f83-8d64-0d8637dc6100" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d18ab9c-c3ef-5157-b965-0edbcd2a01b5" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-7121" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=2({3}^{x})-4?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={3}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/1cd695e6f1fab35a692fde9003d452862db33a894c23657eb87f8d773de8cbb9.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=2({3}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/5a9871ac900e85c9f47118f6233b2e7f094af010dcb73e54221cadac01ce1644.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=2({3}^{x})-4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,-2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/89a7ee023e8d4232b617052efdc9844cb00b2e3f05968119cea11c89242ca61a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79daf9dd-814d-562b-b3bc-b066bd2dfb90" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029ed75f-2824-55cd-b8d6-b2a75f296558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3({3}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243205} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1bcf93-2f4f-5e5f-8434-2c025ebe5427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243206} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5cd200-4684-5f43-9b72-ae77c68a24ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243207} {:answer/id #uuid "919a2f6a-ef93-52d5-becc-65a848322eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243208} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c71fde-4b3c-5a5b-9fb5-e83fffe178ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2({3}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "919a2f6a-ef93-52d5-becc-65a848322eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243208} :answer-field/id #uuid "b291800a-4d91-5ab0-95f9-5267841bd747" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbca085f-0b60-5f9a-bdc1-1da6491170fe" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-7230" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/8764149ca0000522277d48bf48f4ef14ba06f7e4793ba1a58ef639ff7af3a3b9.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={3}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={3}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={3}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2.$ Therefore, $c=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79daf9dd-814d-562b-b3bc-b066bd2dfb90" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57b9bd60-8c58-5dc3-bdee-8306504f945b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243212} {:answer/id #uuid "1abc720b-76d7-5a44-83b6-aba1198c110a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3({2}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243213} {:answer/id #uuid "da89cf2c-f46a-5d5e-ade2-85fe5f479e30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243214} {:answer/id #uuid "f08aca81-a7c8-5d11-b2bb-fa06a3485827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243215} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5ba208-6950-549f-bfab-b886eb7da104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da89cf2c-f46a-5d5e-ade2-85fe5f479e30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243214} :answer-field/id #uuid "487495b4-1ee3-5eff-9a7c-854765a3a157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb2d08ab-7e1f-579d-8314-e037ebc00133" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-7232" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/2ad72b3df34f32d6a76f79f6801887bd6fe1d762fefd74071517a754b1890ac2.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={2}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={2}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={2}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$ Therefore, $c=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "953b6933-1cff-5ebc-91df-e58b50437bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243219} {:answer/id #uuid "45b0c147-a8e6-5f6f-9692-24d80337ec17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243220} {:answer/id #uuid "d7028a03-2d73-55d3-93cb-01575460b9b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243221} {:answer/id #uuid "844f2490-7b12-529a-b80e-0c35a7d52ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243222} {:answer/id #uuid "72d344a4-e544-5a9f-8903-dc30db064235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2({2}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7028a03-2d73-55d3-93cb-01575460b9b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243221} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5f1e821-3bea-5481-bc5e-f76e477663a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b95ee2c-0cc4-59f6-8547-b33a98043811" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-7239" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/1ca11413bbce2ff307e494fb5a79028535b76cff24619e6921620f88c3e274ec.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1,$ so $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})-1.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})-1 \\\\ 2 & =k({2}^{0})-1 \\\\ 2 & =k(1)-1 \\\\ 2 & =k-1 \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=3({2}^{x})-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5806e5-2e0f-5244-a9aa-7cdb5097ca5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243226} {:answer/id #uuid "97533285-8f4f-56bd-b83f-f3b1b21902f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0.5({2}^{x})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243227} {:answer/id #uuid "177318a1-05ec-52c5-aec7-32aeff92cac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243228} {:answer/id #uuid "c67b9555-084a-5b2e-8202-f86d143dca3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243229} {:answer/id #uuid "95745737-dab7-561a-8070-bddded3e150d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0.5({2}^{x})+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c67b9555-084a-5b2e-8202-f86d143dca3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243229} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0d6e266-0b21-5b1a-a2e7-a2047b441b93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "182d5946-6529-5e97-abc0-0f71c01ee4ef" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-7241" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/3a78c4fc61bbf60e1cdfdba6303ecc1a9d5e09caa21d0879556106893a931a09.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4,$ so $c=4.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})+4.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,5.5),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=5.5$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})+4 \\\\ 5.5 & =k({2}^{0})+4 \\\\ 5.5 & =k(1)+4 \\\\ 5.5 & =k+4 \\\\ 1.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=1.5({2}^{x})+4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1f12abb-bb8a-5fc4-8f1d-bc9e90e19137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3