tion/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/929706c015fd38e83c9529a28203584a4e3e53a204ac3b4d20539d090a3fd25a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={12}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={2}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={12}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$ Therefore, $c=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15183709-63f9-51df-a095-6afdd1af6c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({5}^{x})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243254} {:answer/id #uuid "915f2e1e-8564-572c-8f63-c1af64672b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2({5}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243255} {:answer/id #uuid "aecfff04-64b7-576b-8272-922641e623cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2({5}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243256} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c584c4-c3f1-5de4-abda-bb5ed8a7a059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4({5}^{x})-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243257} {:answer/id #uuid "69f068cd-f63c-55e1-bc29-2be69780860d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({5}^{x})-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69f068cd-f63c-55e1-bc29-2be69780860d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({5}^{x})-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243258} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7902bcb-9113-5828-9abe-629de31c8d10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57080f07-951e-52c6-9845-629796cf8644" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-99778" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/5119e42566eff8078bdcf38d8c521e291f902bf1f03f6ddb00321989e3c94c5a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({5}^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({5}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2,$ so $c=-2.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({5}^{x})-2.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({5}^{x})-2 \\\\ 1 & =k({5}^{0})-2 \\\\ 1 & =k(1)-2 \\\\ 1 & =k-2 \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=3({5}^{x})-2.$"}]}
